
📌 Tech Stack Used
    • CI / Automation: GitHub Actions
      
    • Security & Auth: Gitleaks, SonarQube, AWS OIDC (OpenID Connect), Trivy
      
    • Container & Registry: Docker (Multi-stage builds), Amazon ECR (Immutable SHA tags)
      
    • Deployment & GitOps: Helm, Argo CD, Argo Rollouts, Amazon EKS
      
    • Observability & Health: Prometheus, AWS ALB
      
🛠️ Step-by-Step CI/CD Pipeline Breakdown
1. Branching & PR Checks
    • Avoid simple "push, build, deploy" explanations.
      
    • When a developer opens a Pull Request (PR) against the main branch, automated pre-merge PR checks trigger:
      
    ◦ Secrets Scanning: Gitleaks prevents hardcoded credentials/secrets from entering the repository.
          
    ◦ Code Quality & Static Analysis: SonarQube quality gates verify code smells, security vulnerabilities, and coverage threshold.
          
    ◦ Dependency Scanning: Scans third-party packages for known vulnerabilities before merging.
          
2. Post-Merge CI & Authentication
    • AWS Authentication via OIDC: Instead of storing long-lived AWS Access Keys in GitHub Secrets, use OpenID Connect (OIDC) to request short-lived IAM roles securely from AWS.
      
3. Packaging & Container Security
    • Multi-Stage Docker Builds: Keeps container image sizes minimal and secure by separating build dependencies from the runtime environment.
      
    • Image Vulnerability Scanning: Trivy scans container images for vulnerabilities prior to pushing.
      
    • Registry Tagging Strategy: Push images to Amazon ECR using immutable Git SHA tags instead of using latest.
      
4. GitOps & Staging Deployment
    • Build Once, Deploy Many: The same container image tag tested in non-prod moves forward to production—never rebuild code between environments.
      
    • GitOps Workflow: Argo CD continuously monitors the Git repository containing Helm charts/manifests.
      
    • Staging Auto-Deployment: Merges to the main branch update the staging environment manifests automatically via Argo CD.
      
5. Production Canary Deployment & Database Management
    • Approval Gates: Deploying to production requires an approved PR with code owners (CODEOWNERS) review.
      
    • Canary Deployment with Argo Rollouts: Gradually shifts live user traffic (e.g., starting at a 10% split) via AWS Application Load Balancer (ALB).
      
    • Database Migrations: Schema changes are applied carefully before or during rollout according to backward-compatibility strategies.
      
6. Automated Rollback & Observability
    • Prometheus Metrics: Monitors deployment health indicators like error rates, latency, and standard metrics during the canary deployment.
      
    • Automated Rollback: If metrics exceed acceptable thresholds during the canary split, Argo Rollouts automatically triggers a rollback to the previous stable revision.
      
