Step-by-Step Structure to Follow :

1. Branching & Pre-Merge PR Checks
Explain that code changes begin with a Pull Request (PR) against the main branch, triggering automated GitHub Actions checks:

    • Secrets Scanning: Using tools like Gitleaks to catch hardcoded keys or tokens before merging.
      
    • Code Quality & Static Analysis: SonarQube quality gates to enforce test coverage, catch code smells, and detect security vulnerabilities.
      
    • Dependency Scanning: Identifying vulnerable third-party libraries.
      
2. Authentication & Post-Merge CI
Highlight secure authentication and pipeline execution upon merging to main:

    • Short-Lived AWS Authentication: Use AWS OIDC (OpenID Connect) to assume IAM roles dynamically instead of storing static, long-lived AWS Access Keys in GitHub Secrets.
      
3. Containerization & Registry Management
Detail how application artifacts are built, secured, and stored:

    • Multi-Stage Docker Builds: Keeps image sizes small and minimizes attack surfaces by omitting build-time dependencies from final images.
      
    • Vulnerability Scanning: Scanning container images using Trivy before pushing.
      
    • Immutable Registry Tags: Pushing images to Amazon ECR using unique, immutable Git SHA tags instead of mutating the latest tag.
      
4. GitOps & Staging Deployment
Emphasize the "Build Once, Deploy Many" principle—the exact same image tag tested in staging is promoted to production without rebuilding.

    • GitOps State Sync: Using Argo CD to continuously monitor a dedicated Git repository holding Helm charts/manifests.
      
    • Automated Staging Deployments: Merging code automatically updates staging environment manifests, which Argo CD syncs to Amazon EKS.
      
5. Production Canary Deployment & Governance
Demonstrate control and safe release strategies in production:

    • Strict Approvals: Requiring an approved PR with explicit CODEOWNERS sign-off to update production manifests.
      
    • Canary Releases: Deploying with Argo Rollouts to gradually shift user traffic (e.g., starting with a 10% split via AWS ALB) to monitor initial stability.
      
    • Database Migrations: Running schema migrations prior to or during the rollout using backward-compatible approaches.
      
6. Observability & Automated Rollbacks
Show how reliability is maintained post-deployment:

    • Real-time Monitoring: Tracking real-time metrics (error rates, latency spikes) using Prometheus during the canary split.
      
    • Automated Rollback: If metrics cross safe thresholds, Argo Rollouts automatically aborts the canary and reverts to the last stable release.
      
